F201 — a member read through a three-deep alias chain is rejected

baseContribution followed the base's typedef exactly one hop, so `struct D : Port::BaseC` with
`using BaseA = Base; using BaseB = BaseA; using BaseC = BaseB;` resolved to the *name* `Port::BaseB`,
which names no struct. The base then contributed no fields and every member reached through it read
as `unsupported member read`. Direct and one-deep spellings worked, which is why only alias depth
told the three cases apart.

Follows the chain to a fixed point under the same 8-hop bound the other alias walks use. An identity
alias returns nothing (see the F210 fix), so a self-referential `using` terminates.

Note: the hunk adding ALIAS_HOPS also adds EMPTY_STRUCT_BINDINGS, which the F211 patch uses.
Apply F201 before F211, or apply both.

Measured: AliasBase both backends `42 0 0 0 | 42 0 0 0 | 42 0 0 0`, stateSize 96. Suite 1085/0.

